# Line endings are a correctness matter here, not a style one.
#
# Paddock is developed on Windows and on a Mac, and its shell scripts run on
# Linux and macOS (`apps/macos/scripts` included). A shell script
# that picks up CRLF fails at the shebang with "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env
# bash^M", which reads as a missing interpreter rather than a mangled file - and
# it happens silently, because an editor on Windows hands you CRLF without
# asking. It has already happened here: a shell script left the tree as a MIXED
# file, LF on the old lines and CRLF on the new ones, and it still looked fine
# in every Windows tool.
#
# NOTE THE ABSENCE of a global `* text=auto`. It is the usual advice and it is
# wrong for this repo: the index is already 18.5k files at LF, so the rule would
# buy nothing, and it would ask git to reconsider every one of them - a
# whole-repo renormalisation is not something to hand a colleague mid-rebase.
# Pin what actually breaks instead.

# MUST be LF wherever they land - an interpreter reads the shebang byte for byte.
*.sh          text eol=lf
*.bash        text eol=lf
*.py          text eol=lf

# Read by a Linux container, so the same rule applies. Docker itself tolerates
# CRLF in a Dockerfile, but anything it hands to a shell - a heredoc, a
# multi-line RUN - carries the CR straight through into the command.
Dockerfile    text eol=lf
Dockerfile.*  text eol=lf
*.dockerfile  text eol=lf

# Native to Windows, where they are only ever run.
*.ps1         text eol=crlf
*.cmd         text eol=crlf
*.bat         text eol=crlf

# Legal artifacts. The notices generator writes LF and its check mode compares
# byte for byte, so a clone that rewrote the endings would report the file as
# stale on Windows when nobody had touched it.
LICENSE              text eol=lf
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES  text eol=lf

# Chat-template fixtures are byte-exact captures of GGUF metadata, and the
# template tests assert the RENDERED OUTPUT byte for byte - a CRLF rewrite
# quietly changes what is being tested (gemma4_chat_template.jinja was already
# CRLF in Windows working copies when this was pinned).
crates/paddock-runner/tests/fixtures/*.jinja  text eol=lf

# The Tables examples the macOS app bundles are the same bytes the web Studio
# builds, and tables.test.ts compares the two exactly - a Windows checkout
# rewrote the CSVs to CRLF and the parity test failed on files nobody touched.
apps/macos/Sources/PaddockConversationCore/Resources/*.csv  text eol=lf

# Binary, never touched.
*.gguf        binary
*.dll         binary
*.so          binary
*.dylib       binary
*.exe         binary
*.png         binary
*.ico         binary
*.jpg         binary
*.gif         binary
*.webm        binary
*.wav         binary
*.pdf         binary
*.tiff        binary
*.wasm        binary
